    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. what about the 'friction' aspect specifically though? like, when you have those sudden bottlenecks or unexpected surges in congestion that dont seem to follow the capacity math, what's usually the technical cause for those interruptions in flow?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "These unexpected flow interruptions, often called \"spillback\" or \"gridlock,\" are technically caused by the interaction between driver behavior and localized saturation. In signals, an upstream queue blocking an intersection prevents the downstream lane from clearing, creating a shockwave. Roundabouts can suffer from this if entry capacity is exceeded, leading to circulating congestion that effectively locks up the circulatory roadway despite the design.",
      "source": "target",
